{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.00392ee923","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-76","passage":"<p>The ship's logbook, recovered from the wreck in remarkable ______ details of the voyage.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["condition; reveals","condition—reveals","condition reveals","condition, reveals"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The phrase “recovered from the wreck in remarkable condition” is extra information about “the ship's logbook.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.6df26319e6","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-80","passage":"<p>The tallest peak in the range, a jagged mountain known to climbers as the ______ first climbed in 1938.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Needle—was","Needle; was","Needle, was","Needle was"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase “a jagged mountain known to climbers as the Needle” is extra information about “the tallest peak in the range.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.9f7127dde1","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-1","passage":"<p>The recipe requires only four ______, water, salt, and yeast.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["ingredients: flour","ingredients, flour","ingredients; flour","ingredients flour"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The recipe requires only four ingredients” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.5012458b73","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-10","passage":"<p>The school offers three foreign ______, Mandarin, and French.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["languages, Spanish","languages Spanish","languages; Spanish","languages: Spanish"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The school offers three foreign languages” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.41ba41bf13","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-27","passage":"<p>The mission had one main ______ rock samples from the crater's rim.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["objective: collecting","objective collecting","objective; collecting","objective, and collecting"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The mission had one main objective” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.e1ad991be1","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-54","passage":"<p>The chef insists on a single ______ ingredients only.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["rule, and fresh","rule; fresh","rule: fresh","rule fresh"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The chef insists on a single rule” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."}
